  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nFor the rest of the day, expect mild and dry conditions across \nthe Northland with temperatures topping out in the 50s under \nmostly sunny skies. A fast-moving system will still move through\ntonight accompanied by some light precipitation. Temperatures \nwithin this band will remain above freezing, so all rain is the \nprecipitation type expected. Rainfall amounts will be light, \ngenerally a tenth of an inch or less, favoring areas along and \nsouth of the Iron Range.\n\nTemperatures will take a nose dive after the system passes\ntomorrow, with highs only in the upper 30s and low 40s.\nAs cold air advection continues, lake effect precipitation\nexpected to linger after the band passes will slowly\ntransition from rain, to a mix, to eventually all snow\nWednesday night into Thursday. The most likely areas to see\nthis transition will be along the South Shore in northwest\nWisconsin, where northerly winds will funnel colder air\nover the warmer waters of Lake Superior. Any snow that falls \nWednesday into Thursday is expected to melt on most surfaces,\nbut may accumulate on elevated surfaces. Snow totals have\ndecreased slightly, with most accumulations at 1.5\" or less\nacross the South Shore snow belt. However, any snow that manages\nto accumulate will not last long as temperatures climb back \ninto the mid 30s during the day. Lake effect snow is expected to\ndissipate Thursday night into Friday, though some flurries are \npossible on and off throughout the weekend as cold air advection\ncontinues over Lake Superior. Elsewhere across the region, \nThursday and Friday will be mainly dry and chilly with overnight\nlows dropping into the 20s.\n\nBy the weekend, upper level ridging will begin to build\neastward from the Plains. The Northland will see quiet weather \nwith temperatures gradually modifying into the 40s by Sunday. \nHigh pressure moves over the area early next week, and southerly\nflow will cause temperatures to climb back into the 50s for \nmore above normal temperatures before more chances for \nprecipitation in the middle of the week.",
